Player identity: the LSDP node id against the /SyncStatus mac attribute.

"The id is the player's MAC and it's the cache key a controller dedupes on" is
about LSDP, and that is already covered (§12.1): the announce carries a 6-byte
nodeId that is a MAC, unique per node rather than per interface, and it is the
correct cache key. Nothing to resolve there.

The open question is the other identifiers. /SyncStatus carries id="<ip>:<port>"
and a separate mac attribute, and <slave> entries use a bare IP as id. Is that
mac attribute the same value as the LSDP node id, or the MAC of whichever
interface happens to be up?

It matters because these players have two MACs, one per interface, and they
switch between them: Wi-Fi is disabled when Ethernet is connected and returns
when the cable is pulled. I have observed that, and it is documented vendor
behaviour. The controller app is documented as showing the MAC of the connected
card, so at least one surface follows the active interface. If /SyncStatus does
the same, anything keyed on it sees a player change identity on a cable pull,
where LSDP would not.

Test: read the mac attribute and an LSDP announce for one player on Ethernet,
then again on Wi-Fi, and compare all three values. This probably belongs in the
claim register rather than here.

Separately, worth knowing: if the cable is already connected when the wired
network starts working, a player may stay on Wi-Fi until the cable is
replugged.
